 ## Background
-The integration of monocular depth priors into NeRF optimization emerged to address the limitations of sparse-view reconstruction, where photometric loss alone leads to shape-radiance ambiguity and floating artifacts. Early works like NerfingMVS [S2] and MonoSDF [S3] demonstrated that external depth cues could significantly improve geometric accuracy. Subsequent research, such as DäRF [S1, unofficial], shifted from static supervision to adaptive adaptation, where the MDE network itself is fine-tuned to the target scene, ensuring multi-view consistency. More recent approaches like GAINS [S1, unofficial] and DiffDP-based methods [S1, unofficial] incorporate uncertainty estimation and diffusion-based priors to handle noise and ambiguity in sparse inputs.
+The integration of monocular depth priors into NeRF optimization emerged to address the limitations of sparse-view reconstruction, where photometric consistency alone is insufficient to disambiguate geometry and appearance [S1, unofficial][S2]. Early works like NerfingMVS demonstrated that fine-tuning monocular depth estimators on sparse SfM points could guide volume sampling [S2]. Subsequent methods such as DäRF and NeRF-guided MDE Adaptation introduced joint optimization strategies, allowing the MDE network to adapt to the specific scene geometry captured by the NeRF, thereby resolving ambiguities like convexity/concavity confusion [S1, unofficial]. More recent approaches have explored distributional losses like Earth Mover’s Distance to handle the uncertainty in monocular priors [S1, unofficial], and extended supervision to unseen viewpoints to ensure global geometric coherence [S1, unofficial].
 
